WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.861 position:50% size:92% align:center
The starter doubles five input values.

00:00:03.081 --> 00:00:06.512 position:50% size:92% align:center
Follow input[2] to output[2]. How does the

00:00:06.512 --> 00:00:08.799 position:50% size:92% align:center
thread choose that position?

00:00:10.119 --> 00:00:12.336 position:50% size:92% align:center
The supplied setup already copies

00:00:12.336 --> 00:00:14.151 position:50% size:92% align:center
the inputs into GPU memory.

00:00:14.371 --> 00:00:17.229 position:50% size:92% align:center
Leave that setup unchanged for this experiment.

00:00:18.549 --> 00:00:21.109 position:50% size:92% align:center
We follow block zero’s local thread two.

00:00:21.329 --> 00:00:23.132 position:50% size:92% align:center
Zero times four, plus two,

00:00:23.132 --> 00:00:24.657 position:50% size:92% align:center
gives array index two.

00:00:25.977 --> 00:00:30.223 position:50% size:92% align:center
The same index selects input[2] and output[2].

00:00:31.543 --> 00:00:35.050 position:50% size:92% align:center
An SM, or streaming multiprocessor, is execution

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.876 position:50% size:92% align:center
hardware on the GPU chip.

00:00:37.096 --> 00:00:39.485 position:50% size:92% align:center
It runs the selected thread’s instructions.

00:00:40.805 --> 00:00:43.283 position:50% size:92% align:center
The thread reads 3.0 from

00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:45.563 position:50% size:92% align:center
input[2] in GPU memory.

00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:48.087 position:50% size:92% align:center
That value becomes working state for

00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:49.815 position:50% size:92% align:center
its instructions on the SM.

00:00:51.135 --> 00:00:55.423 position:50% size:92% align:center
The SM’s arithmetic hardware multiplies

00:00:55.423 --> 00:00:58.281 position:50% size:92% align:center
3.0 by 2.0, producing 6.0.

00:00:58.501 --> 00:01:00.517 position:50% size:92% align:center
Threads keep their own working values

00:01:00.517 --> 00:01:02.043 position:50% size:92% align:center
while sharing this hardware.

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:05.709 position:50% size:92% align:center
The thread writes 6.0 to

00:01:05.709 --> 00:01:08.056 position:50% size:92% align:center
output[2] in GPU memory.

00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:10.734 position:50% size:92% align:center
Its index fixes the destination even if

00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:12.436 position:50% size:92% align:center
other threads finish first.

00:01:13.756 --> 00:01:16.387 position:50% size:92% align:center
main copies the GPU results into CPU

00:01:16.387 --> 00:01:18.215 position:50% size:92% align:center
memory and prints Output.

00:01:19.535 --> 00:01:22.009 position:50% size:92% align:center
Return to the lesson. Find runKernel

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:23.796 position:50% size:92% align:center
in the CUDA source editor.

00:01:24.016 --> 00:01:28.475 position:50% size:92% align:center
Change its multiplier from 2.0f to 4.0f.

00:01:28.695 --> 00:01:31.535 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause. Predict the third Output value and the

00:01:31.535 --> 00:01:33.303 position:50% size:92% align:center
hardware doing the multiply.

00:01:33.523 --> 00:01:35.767 position:50% size:92% align:center
Click Compile and run. Return to this

00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:37.768 position:50% size:92% align:center
recording to compare your result.

00:01:43.588 --> 00:01:48.539 position:50% size:92% align:center
With 4.0f, input[2] still supplies 3.0

00:01:48.539 --> 00:01:52.057 position:50% size:92% align:center
and output[2] becomes 12.0.

00:01:52.277 --> 00:01:55.380 position:50% size:92% align:center
The same index chooses the destination; the SM

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.269 position:50% size:92% align:center
still performs the multiply.

00:01:58.589 --> 00:02:01.538 position:50% size:92% align:center
The index selects data. The SM performs

00:02:01.538 --> 00:02:04.563 position:50% size:92% align:center
arithmetic. GPU memory holds the arrays.

00:02:04.783 --> 00:02:07.108 position:50% size:92% align:center
Lesson three explores the launch size.
